# Agent Coding Guidelines

This document outlines coding conventions that must be followed when working on this codebase.

## C# Conventions

### C# - Prefer Dependency Injection over Static Classes

When writing or refactoring C# code:

- Prefer dependency injection (constructor injection) for services, repositories, helpers, and shared logic.
- Avoid making classes static unless there is a clear, justified reason (e.g. pure utility functions with no state, extension methods, or true singletons with no dependencies).
- Do not default to static classes or static methods for business logic, data access, or anything that could benefit from testability or configurability.
- If a class has any dependencies, state, or needs mocking in tests → use proper DI instead of static.
- Static classes are acceptable only for stateless, pure helper methods (like Math or String extensions).

Favor clean, injectable classes with interfaces where it improves maintainability and testability.